@startuml 19_timing_i2c_bitbang

title I2C at bit level: START, two data bits, clock stretching, ACK, STOP

scale 10 as 70 pixels

binary "SCL" as SCL
binary "SDA" as SDA
concise "Phase" as PH

@0
SCL is high
SDA is high
PH is "idle"

@10
SDA is low
PH is "S"

@20
SCL is low
PH is "bit 1"

@25
SDA is high

@40
SCL is low
PH is "bit 0"

@45
SDA is low

@50
PH is "stretch"

@62
PH is "bit 0"

@72
PH is "ACK"

@74
SDA is high

@77
SDA is low

@92
PH is "P"

@110
SDA is high
PH is "idle"

@30
SCL is high

@62
SCL is high

@72
SCL is low

@82
SCL is high

@92
SCL is low

@102
SCL is high

highlight 50 to 62 #LightPink : target holds SCL low

note bottom of PH
  S (START, t=10): SDA falls while SCL is high. Only START and STOP may change SDA while SCL is high.
  bit 1 (t=20..40): master releases SDA while SCL is low (t=25); target samples while SCL is high (t=30..40).
  bit 0 (t=40..72): master pulls SDA low (t=45) and releases SCL at t=50, but the target holds SCL low
      until t=62 (clock stretching). The master must read SCL back and wait; it samples only after SCL is really high.
  ACK (t=72..92): master releases SDA (rises through the pull-up at t=74); target pulls it low (t=77);
      master samples SDA = 0 while SCL is high (t=82..92): acknowledged.
  P (STOP, t=110): SDA rises while SCL is high.
end note
@enduml
